        "tiff-6-0-baseline-mutate"
      ],
      "license": "MIT OR Apache-2.0",
      "testOnly": true,
      "productionReachable": false,
      "networkDuringExecution": false,
      "homepage": "https://github.com/image-rs/image-tiff",
      "rationale": "📖️ The case's oracle role: `tiff` 0.11's `Decoder::find_tag` reads the real scan's five Baseline axes out of IFD 0 — Compression (259), PhotometricInterpretation (262), BitsPerSample (258), TileWidth/TileLength (322/323), StripOffsets (273) — its raster decodes through `read_image`, and its IFD count comes from walking `more_images`/`next_image`. `../🦀️.rs` applies each kind to those axes as Adobe TIFF 6.0 Part 1 defines the field and reads the class off the specification's own tables, never consulting this repository's decoder, snapshot or checker. The comparison is on the conformance projection (`ordered-json-v1`), because this repository's encoder regenerates every strip tag and four of the kinds are therefore not byte-observable. The same crate is verified and registered for the sibling 🧾️document subset.",
      "comparisonProfiles": [
        "semantic-raster-v1",
        "ordered-json-v1"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "noOracleDecisions": [
    {
      "id": "tiff-6-0-baseline-round-trip-normalization",
      "capabilities": [
        "tiff-6-0-baseline-round-trip"
      ],
      "rationale": "The decode/re-encode law of the Baseline subset is a statement about THIS repository's encoder: `encode_tiff` regenerates every `CORE_STRIP_TAGS` entry from the raster it writes, so the bytes it produces are defined by its own strip layout and no third-party writer can be asked to reproduce them. The case judges the round trip by laws instead — byte equality with the reference writer's committed file, a flipped decoded byte changing the output, Baseline membership after the round trip — and by the independent `image` reader registered for the 🧾️document subset, which must read the same geometry on both sides.",
      "substitutes": [
        "metamorphic-laws"
      ]
    }
  ],
